      "observed": "On the FCC Peripherals sheet the PWM driver U2 (PCA9685PW) carries the annotation 'I2C address: 0x41' (its A0 address pin is tied high), and its SCL/SDA pins 26/27 are wired to I2C2_SCL_FCC / I2C2_SDA_FCC. The current/voltage sensor U1 (INA219AIDCNR) is on the same I2C2 bus (pins 5/6 to I2C2_SCL_FCC / I2C2_SDA_FCC) with A0=A1=GND, i.e. address 0x40.",
      "expected": "The design specification's component table states the PWM driver is 'NXP PCA9685PW (I2C 16-ch PWM), I2C addr 0x40' and the current sensor is 'TI INA219AIDCNR, I2C addr 0x40'. Two devices cannot both sit at 0x40 on one bus, so the intended design must place them on separate buses with PCA9685 at 0x40. The build instead merges PCA9685 onto the INA219's I2C2 bus and bumps its address to 0x41, deviating from the specified 0x40.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "FCCPeripherals sheet, U2 PCA9685PW: 'I2C address: 0x41'; U2 pin26->I2C2_SCL_FCC, pin27->I2C2_SDA_FCC"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Power sheet, U1 INA219AIDCNR: 'I2C address: 0x40'; pin5->I2C2_SCL_FCC, pin6->I2C2_SDA_FCC"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "Component Table: 'PWM/servo driver ... NXP PCA9685PW (I2C 16-ch PWM), I2C addr 0x40' and 'Current/voltage sensor ... TI INA219AIDCNR, I2C addr 0x40'"}
      ],
      "impact": "Firmware written against the reference design (PCA9685 at 0x40) will not find the servo/PWM driver at that address, so PWM outputs to the servos fail; the build also relocates the PWM driver onto the INA219's I2C2 bus rather than the bus implied by the spec.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The board otherwise reproduces the specification faithfully (correct MCUs, sensor set, regulator parts, shunt/inductor/feedback values, and the documented barometer RESET/EOC pin mix-up). The one drawing-vs-spec conflict is the PCA9685 PWM driver: the schematic sets it to I2C address 0x41 on the same I2C2 bus as the INA219 (0x40), whereas the specification states the PWM driver should be at 0x40, so the servo driver is not at the intended address."
}

